--TEST--
A `[db.<name>]` block naming `sqlite` opens off its own arm of § 2's discriminated union — a file where the other four have an address — and § 4's `execute`, `executeMany` and `query` run over it — `rule:core-classes/db-connection-is-named`, `rule:core-classes/db-statement-members`, `rule:core-classes/db-parameters` and `rule:core-classes/db-column-types`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The only `Core\Db` case in this tree that reaches a *running* database.
// SQLite needs no server and no container, so `:memory:` is a path like any
// other and the statements below are really prepared, bound and stepped —
// which is what makes this the case that would catch a driver wired up in the
// registry and not behind it.
var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");

// § 4: `execute` answers a `Write` and not a count. On this backend both of its
// numbers come off the connection rather than off the statement, which is
// SQLite's own rule — `sqlite3_changes` and `sqlite3_last_insert_rowid` — so a
// `create table` reports the change count it inherited rather than an absence.
var $made = $db->execute("create table t (id integer primary key, name text)", []);
echo "created ", $made->affected(), "\n";

// § 5's markers, bound as SQLite storage classes rather than rendered into
// text: `nvs_db::sqlite::encode` is where a `string` becomes `TEXT` instead of
// octets on a wire.
var $wrote = $db->execute("insert into t (name) values (?)", ["ada"]);
echo "inserted ", $wrote->affected(), " at ", $wrote->lastId() ?? 0, "\n";

// § 4's batch: one prepare, one execution per set, the counts summed — and not
// a transaction, which is the same rule it is on the other four drivers.
var $many = $db->executeMany("insert into t (name) values (?)", [["grace"], ["alan"]]);
echo "batched ", $many, "\n";

// § 4's read. The bound value is compared by the engine and not by a rewrite of
// the statement, so the row `alan` wrote is the one missing below.
var $rows = $db->query("select name from t where name <> ? order by id", ["alan"]);
echo "read ", $rows->count(), "\n";

// § 9 keys this backend's column map off what the *schema* declared, since a
// value's storage class says nothing about what it was meant as. `name` was
// declared `text`.
var $columns = $rows->columns();
echo "column ", $columns[0]->name(), " is ", $columns[0]->type() == Core\Db\ColumnType::Text ? "text" : "something else", "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# § 3: a SQLite connection's file path comes from its config block, so
# `db.connect` covers it and there is no address for `rule:http-server/allow-url-pins-the-address`'s table to have
# an opinion about.
[capabilities.db]
connect = ["main"]

[db.main]
driver = "sqlite"
path = ":memory:"
--EXPECT--
created 0
inserted 1 at 1
batched 2
read 2
column name is text
